 ## Background
-The concept of using analytic distance functions for rendering was popularized by Inigo Quilez in his online tutorials . These primitives form the basis of Raymarching, a technique where rays are stepped through space using the distance function as a lower bound on the distance to the scene geometry. The Sphere SDF is particularly notable for enabling adaptive step sizes that guarantee surface intersection without overshooting .
+Signed Distance Functions for basic primitives were popularized in computer graphics by Martin Held and later refined by Ian McEwan, Andrew Kensler, and others for real-time ray marching . The sphere SDF is among the oldest and most widely used implicit primitives due to its simplicity and rotational symmetry . The box SDF requires careful implementation to avoid underestimation in corner regions, a pitfall noted in early implementations .
 
